
[center][b][size=4]This is an extremely complicated mod, there are likely still some issues I haven't found, but I have found no game breaking issues after more than 40 hours of testing. Also I'm stuck on steamdeck for the next few weeks so please upload your own pics and videos![/size]
[/b]
[/center]
[line]

Programmable follower behaviour. Every follower carries ordered lists of [i]Gambits[/i], which are [b][Condition] -> [Action][/b] rules, first match wins, authored live in game and per follower. It's Final Fantasy XII's gambit system rebuilt on Skyrim's own actor primitives. Followers cast, attack, heal, drink, restock, loot, [i]walk[/i] to loot, and trade at merchants, selling their junk and buying the supplies they run short on. Nothing is scripted to a specific follower, so it works on anyone your framework recognises as a teammate, vanilla or modded.

The whole thing is built on Linux with no Creation Kit and no xEdit. The plugin is generated byte by byte from Python, the install-time patcher is a Mutagen/.NET (Synthesis) tool, and the SKSE plugin builds on GitHub Actions.

[size=5][b]Features[/b][/size]

[b]Two tables, not one.[/b] Each follower carries independent [i]combat[/i] and [i]logistics[/i] rule lists with their own slots, and they never interleave. It's top-down, first match wins, one action per tick. A follower can't heal and attack in the same cycle, so the cost of a heal is the attack he didn't make. The list is yours. Nothing reorders it, nothing dedupes it, and followers don't coordinate with each other.

[b]Cast where you mean it.[/b] A cast rule aims where you point it. A foe, a hurt ally, the player, a specific follower, or [b]Auto[/b], which picks the target based on what the spell does. A heal goes to whoever's worst off and works down the party as they recover, and a buff like Candlelight lights the whole group. Heals channel the way they should now. They top a target off and stop, they quit the moment the caster is out of magicka, and they won't spam or drain him dry. Followers cast on themselves too, so self-heals, wards, and things like [i]when dark -> Candlelight[/i]. And if you tell one to power-attack a foe who's blocking, he closes the distance and hits that foe instead of swinging at air.

[b]Followers loot like people, not vacuums.[/b] They [i]walk[/i] over to loot instead of teleport-grabbing it across the room, they pick locks they're skilled enough for, and they leave the gear and gold on a fresh corpse for a few seconds first (your pick). They won't take owned goods, they hold off entirely while you're sneaking, and they won't loot inside a player home unless you opt in. Upgrades are judged by category and metric, never by the specific item, so an archer won't swap to a mace and a light-armour follower won't grab plate. Modded gear works with no patch.

[b]Followers trade at merchants.[/b] Stand a follower next to a vendor and he'll sell his unworn junk, highest value first, capped by the vendor's own gold, and buy the supplies he's run short on out of his own purse, picking from what that vendor [i]actually stocks[/i]. It's off by default. Turn on [b]Follower economy[/b] in the MCM.

[b]Confidence, on an invisible string.[/b] How far a follower ranges from you is a live readout of how confident he is to survive alone right now. Healthy in an easy zone, he pushes ahead and ranges out to loot. Hurt, or in a hard fight, he pulls back and fights at your side. Badly outmatched, he falls back to you on his own. You never see a number. You read it through behaviour.

[b]Reactions are human, not robotic.[/b] A gambit decision is a choice reaction, and in people that runs about 300 to 600 ms, so the responses are drawn from a right-skewed distribution instead of firing on a metronome. Followers miss a beat now and then. Higher Rapport ranks visibly hesitate less.

[b]Progression through Rapport.[/b] Rule slots are earned by fighting alongside that [i]specific[/i] follower. It's never pooled or transferable, and it survives dismissal, so you can leave someone in Breezehome for two hundred hours and they're exactly as you left them. What a follower [i]can[/i] be told to do comes from his own skills and spellbook. A battlemage opens up cast actions a pure warrior never will.

[b]The Field Orders board.[/b] You author the rules in game, per follower, on the Field Orders power. It's a native menu, four skins, full controller support, with a Followers roster, the Gambits editor, and a plain-language summary of every rule. A passive HUD shows each follower's rank, rapport, live vitals, and distance while you fight. The rest of the settings live in an MCM, including the Behaviour Layer page for the confidence leash and walk-to-loot.

[b]Layered on vanilla AI, never replacing it.[/b] A follower with no matching rule behaves byte-for-byte like one without MFO installed. No no-op package, no neutral command, no engine call at all. MFO drives followers through runtime AI instead of record edits, so it doesn't conflict with follower mods, AI overhauls, or combat mods.

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]SKSE64[/*]
[*]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/*]
[*]SkyUI[/*]
[*]MCM Helper (version 9 or newer)[/*]
[*]powerofthree's Papyrus Extender. This one is only needed for the optional follower economy (selling and buying). Everything else runs without it.[/*]
[*][url=https://github.com/Mutagen-Modding/Synthesis]Synthesis[/url]. MFO ships a Synthesis patcher that builds an item catalog from your load order, so gambit categories (potions, ammo, gear classes) cover modded items with no per-mod patch (see Install). Synthesis carries its own .NET requirement, so there's nothing extra to install for MFO.[/*]
[*]No DLC required.[/*]
[/list]
[b]Anniversary Edition (1.6.x) is recommended.[/b] The [i]walk-to-it[/i] behaviours (walking to loot, Flee to player, and Auto-retreat) ride a native alias fill that's only verified on AE, so on Special Edition (1.5.97) they're inactive. Loot is picked up at arm's reach, and flee and retreat do nothing. Everything else (combat gambits, restocking, looting itself, and the merchant economy) works on SE and AE alike. VR is not supported.

Developed and tested on Skyrim SE 1.6.1170 against a Requiem-based list (LoreRim) and a pure-vanilla Steam Deck install. The bundled MFO.dll is part of the mod and required.

[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]

[b]1.[/b] Install the package with your mod manager as a normal mod, and enable [b]MFO.esp[/b]. Load order is not sensitive.

[b]2.[/b] (Recommended) Adapt MFO's item catalog to your load order with [b]Synthesis[/b]. There's no exe to run:
[list]
[*][b]Easiest:[/b] download the [b]MFO.synth[/b] file (in Optional Files), then in Synthesis select a group and double-click it. It adds the MFO patcher for you, with the correct project already selected.[/*]
[*][b]Or manually:[/b] add a Git Repository patcher pointing to [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/MFO]github.com/marthofdoom/MFO[/url], and select the project [i]installer/MFO.Synthesis/MFO.Synthesis.csproj[/i].[/*]
[/list]
Run your Synthesis pipeline, and re-run it after any load-order change. Without it MFO is still fully playable. It falls back to a shipped catalog, and the patcher just upgrades gambit item coverage to your actual list.

[b]3.[/b] Launch through SKSE. Open the MCM ([b]Mod Configuration -> marth's Follower Overhaul[/b]) to configure. Every behaviour ships off or conservative by default.

[size=5][b]FAQ[/b][/size]

[b]Will it conflict with my follower framework, AI overhaul, or combat mod?[/b]
No. MFO layers on top of whatever manages your followers. It acts on anyone flagged as your teammate, through runtime AI. It edits no follower records and no AI packages of other mods, and a follower with no matching rule is byte-for-byte like one without MFO.

[b]Does it work with modded followers and modded items?[/b]
Yes. Followers are detected by teammate status, not by a list. Item categories (potions, ammo, gear classes) come from the Synthesis-built catalog of your load order, so modded gear is judged by the same class-and-metric rules with no per-mod patch.

[b]Do I have to run Synthesis?[/b]
It's recommended but not mandatory. Without it MFO uses a shipped item catalog. With it the catalog is rebuilt from the mods you actually run.

[b]Why won't my follower cast the spell I set?[/b]
Competence is not permission. If he lacks the magicka or the skill, the rule fails and the next one is tried. The board tells you why, so a list that doesn't work is legible instead of mysterious.

[b]Can I turn parts off or tune it?[/b]
Yes. Everything ships off or conservative. Looting, the economy, auto-retreat, the confidence leash, dibs timing, and the rest are MCM toggles and sliders.

[size=5][b]Roadmap[/b][/size]

The core loop is here. Gambits on both tables, human-feel actuation, looting, the economy, Rapport, and the board. Current directions:

[b]Town errands.[/b] Followers walking to merchants and doors on their own business, without you.

[b]Deeper interop.[/b] Compatibility contracts with follower levelling and equipment-visibility mods as their APIs allow.

